# ============================================================================
# Exports — plain I/O/CLI helpers only.
# ============================================================================
#
# NOTE: `_CliCommands.run_commands`/`_CliCommands.handler_commands`/
# `_CliCompile.*` (cli_run_lint, cli_compile, cli_native_build, etc.) used to
# be re-exported here too, duplicating `app.io.cli_commands` / `app.io.cli_compile`
# (both already split out of this file for the 800-line limit, per their own
# header comments). Those re-exports were leftover from before that split and
# were never used for anything `app.io.cli_commands`/`app.io.cli_compile`
# couldn't already provide directly — but because this file's own header says
# "Pure Simple I/O" and light consumers like `app.io.mod` import only a
# handful of local names from it (e.g. `shell`, `settlement_main`), the Rust
# loader's `export use` flattening pulled in the *entire* compiler (driver,
# lexer, HIR, MIR, every backend) as a transitive dependency of any file that
# imports anything at all from `app.io.mod`/`app.io.cli_ops` — regardless of
# which names were actually requested. That's what made `simple test`/`simple
# run` on specs like `arch_check_spec.spl` relint ~250 compiler-internal files
# every invocation. See
# doc/08_tracking/bug/stage4_test_runner_daemon_fallback_relint_nonmemoized_2026-07-20.md.
# Callers that need the CLI-dispatch/compiler-heavy surface must import it
# directly from `app.io.cli_commands` / `app.io.cli_compile` instead.
export cli_handle_compile, cli_env_get, env_set
